solution:
[Overview]
This is an absolute value linear equation problem. The goal is to find all real y satisfying the equation. The key idea is to split into cases according to the sign of y-6.

[Plan]
P1. Locate the breakpoint y=6 where the absolute value changes sign.
P2. Solve the equation under the condition y >= 6.
P3. Solve the equation under the condition y < 6.
P4. Keep only candidates satisfying their case conditions and verify in the original equation.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] The equation is |y-6| + 2y = 9, and the expression y-6 changes sign at y=6.
S2. [case] Split into the cases y >= 6 and y < 6.
S3. [symbolic] In the case y >= 6, replace |y-6| by y-6 -> {{|y-6| + 2y = 9 => y - 6 + 2y = 9}}
S4. [symbolic] Simplify the equation for y >= 6 -> {{y - 6 + 2y = 9 => 3y = 15}}
S5. [symbolic] Solve the equation for y >= 6 -> {{3y = 15 => y = 5}}
S6. [derive] The candidate y=5 does not satisfy y >= 6, so it is discarded.
S7. [symbolic] In the case y < 6, replace |y-6| by -(y-6) -> {{|y-6| + 2y = 9 => -(y - 6) + 2y = 9}}
S8. [symbolic] Simplify the equation for y < 6 -> {{-(y - 6) + 2y = 9 => y + 6 = 9}}
S9. [symbolic] Solve the equation for y < 6 -> {{y + 6 = 9 => y = 3}}
S10. [derive] The candidate y=3 satisfies y < 6, so it is accepted.

[Check]
V1. Substitute y=3 into the original equation -> <<|3-6|+2*3=9>>
V2. The discarded candidate y=5 fails its case condition and gives |5-6|+2*5=11, not 9.

[Answer]
\boxed{3}

