solution:
[Overview]
This is a radical simplification problem. The goal is to simplify the product of three square-root fractions and rationalize the denominator. The key idea is to combine the radicals, cancel common factors, and then rationalize.

[Plan]
P1. Combine the three radical fractions into a single radical quotient.
P2. Simplify the numerator and denominator and cancel common factors.
P3. Rationalize the denominator of the resulting fraction.
P4. Verify the simplification by a reverse or numerical check.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let E = (sqrt2/sqrt5)*(sqrt3/sqrt6)*(sqrt4/sqrt7).
S2. [symbolic] Combine the radical factors -> {{(sqrt2/sqrt5)*(sqrt3/sqrt6)*(sqrt4/sqrt7) => sqrt(2*3*4)/sqrt(5*6*7)}}
S3. [symbolic] Simplify inside the radicals -> {{sqrt(2*3*4)/sqrt(5*6*7) => sqrt(24)/sqrt(210)}}
S4. [symbolic] Factor the radicals to expose common factors -> {{sqrt(24)/sqrt(210) => sqrt(6*4)/(sqrt(6)*sqrt(35))}}
S5. [symbolic] Cancel the common factor sqrt(6) -> {{sqrt(6*4)/(sqrt(6)*sqrt(35)) => sqrt(4)/sqrt(35)}}
S6. [symbolic] Simplify sqrt(4) -> {{sqrt(4)/sqrt(35) => 2/sqrt(35)}}
S7. [symbolic] Rationalize the denominator -> {{2/sqrt(35) => 2*sqrt(35)/35}}

[Check]
V1. Multiplying 2/sqrt(35) by sqrt(35)/sqrt(35) gives (2*sqrt(35))/35, so the denominator is rationalized.
V2. The original product is approximately 0.338, and 2*sqrt(35)/35 is also approximately 0.338, so the result is consistent.

[Answer]
\boxed{\frac{2\sqrt{35}}{35}}

