solution:
[Overview]
This is a partial fractions problem. The goal is to find constants A and B so that the rational expression decomposes over the factors of the denominator. The key idea is to clear denominators and equate coefficients or evaluate convenient values.

[Plan]
P1. Factor the denominator and set up the partial fraction equation.
P2. Multiply both sides by the factored denominator to get a polynomial identity.
P3. Solve the resulting linear equations for A and B.
P4. Verify the result by recombining the fractions.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Factor the denominator as x^2 - x - 42 = (x - 7)(x + 6), so the identity becomes (3x + 5)/((x - 7)(x + 6)) = A/(x - 7) + B/(x + 6).
S2. [symbolic] Multiply both sides by (x - 7)(x + 6) -> {{3x+5 => A(x+6)+B(x-7)}}
S3. [symbolic] Expand the right side -> {{A(x+6)+B(x-7) => (A+B)x + (6A-7B)}}
S4. [derive] Equate coefficients of x and the constant terms to obtain A + B = 3 and 6A - 7B = 5.
S5. [symbolic] Use B = 3 - A in the second equation -> {{6A-7(3-A)=5 => 13A-21=5}}
S6. [symbolic] Solve for A -> {{13A=26 => A=2}}
S7. [symbolic] Solve for B -> {{B=3-A => B=1}}

[Check]
V1. Substitute A = 2 and B = 1 into the right side and combine -> <<2/(x-7)+1/(x+6)=(3*x+5)/((x-7)*(x+6))>>
V2. The numerator matches 3x + 5 and the denominator matches x^2 - x - 42, so the decomposition is correct.

[Answer]
\boxed{(2,1)}

