solution:
[Overview]
This is a polynomial factorization problem. The goal is to factor x^8 - 256 completely over the integers. The key idea is to use the difference of squares repeatedly and stop when the remaining factors are irreducible over the integers.

[Plan]
P1. Rewrite 256 as a power of 2.
P2. Apply the difference of squares identity to x^8 - 256.
P3. Factor the resulting difference of squares x^4 - 16 again.
P4. Factor the difference of squares x^2 - 4 again.
P5. Identify the remaining quadratic and quartic factors as irreducible over the integers.

[Solution]
S1. [symbolic] Rewrite the constant as a power -> {{x^8 - 256 => x^8 - 2^8}}
S2. [theorem] Use the difference of squares identity: a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b).
S3. [symbolic] Apply the identity with a = x^4 and b = 2^4 -> {{x^8 - 2^8 => (x^4 - 2^4)(x^4 + 2^4)}}
S4. [symbolic] Simplify the powers of 2 -> {{(x^4 - 2^4)(x^4 + 2^4) => (x^4 - 16)(x^4 + 16)}}
S5. [symbolic] Factor x^4 - 16 as a difference of squares -> {{x^4 - 16 => (x^2 - 4)(x^2 + 4)}}
S6. [symbolic] Factor x^2 - 4 as a difference of squares -> {{x^2 - 4 => (x - 2)(x + 2)}}
S7. [symbolic] Combine all factors -> {{(x^2 - 4)(x^2 + 4)(x^4 + 16) => (x - 2)(x + 2)(x^2 + 4)(x^4 + 16)}}
S8. [theorem] Over the integers, x - 2 and x + 2 are linear and irreducible, x^2 + 4 has no integer factorization, and x^4 + 16 has no factorization into lower-degree integer polynomials.

[Check]
V1. Reverse the factorization: (x - 2)(x + 2) = x^2 - 4, then (x^2 - 4)(x^2 + 4) = x^4 - 16, and finally (x^4 - 16)(x^4 + 16) = x^8 - 256.
V2. The factor x^2 + 4 has discriminant -16, so it has no real linear factors; the quartic x^4 + 16 has no integer linear or quadratic factorization, so the integer factorization is complete.

[Answer]
\boxed{(x-2)(x+2)(x^2+4)(x^4+16)}

