solution:
[Overview]
This is a polynomial division problem. The goal is to divide a degree-4 polynomial by a linear polynomial and identify the quotient. The key idea is polynomial long division, tracking the quotient terms and the remainder.

[Plan]
P1. Write the divisor in standard form as 3z + 2.
P2. Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor.
P3. Multiply the divisor by the new quotient term and subtract.
P4. Repeat until the remaining degree is less than the divisor degree.
P5. Collect the quotient terms and verify by multiplication.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let the dividend be P(z) = 3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2 and the divisor be D(z) = 3z + 2.
S2. [symbolic] The first quotient term comes from the leading terms: {{3z^4/(3z) => z^3}}
S3. [symbolic] Subtract z^3(3z + 2) from the dividend: {{(3z^4-4z^3+5z^2-11z+2)-z^3(3z+2) => -6z^3+5z^2-11z+2}}
S4. [symbolic] The next quotient term is {{(-6z^3)/(3z) => -2z^2}}
S5. [symbolic] Subtract (-2z^2)(3z + 2): {{(-6z^3+5z^2-11z+2)-(-2z^2)(3z+2) => 9z^2-11z+2}}
S6. [symbolic] The next quotient term is {{9z^2/(3z) => 3z}}
S7. [symbolic] Subtract 3z(3z + 2): {{(9z^2-11z+2)-3z(3z+2) => -17z+2}}
S8. [symbolic] The next quotient term is {{(-17z)/(3z) => -17/3}}
S9. [symbolic] Subtract (-17/3)(3z + 2): {{(-17z+2)-(-17/3)(3z+2) => 40/3}}
S10. [symbolic] Collect the quotient terms: {{z^3-2z^2+3z-17/3 => z^3-2z^2+3z-\frac{17}{3}}}

[Check]
V1. Multiply the quotient by the divisor and add the remainder: (z^3 - 2z^2 + 3z - 17/3)(3z + 2) + 40/3 = 3z^4 - 4z^3 + 5z^2 - 11z + 2.
V2. The remainder is 40/3, so the quotient polynomial is confirmed.

[Answer]
\boxed{z^3-2z^2+3z-\frac{17}{3}}

[Overview]
This is a complex number simplification problem. The goal is to simplify a high power of a quotient of complex numbers. The key idea is to simplify the quotient first, express it as a power of i, and then use the periodicity of powers of i.

[Plan]
P1. Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator to simplify the quotient.
P2. Use i^2 = -1 to reduce the quotient to a simple power of i.
P3. Use the periodicity of powers of i to evaluate the 1000th power.
P4. Verify the result using the fourth-power period of i.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let z = (1 + i)/(1 - i). We need to simplify z^1000.
S2. [symbolic] Multiply numerator and denominator by 1 + i -> {{(1+i)/(1-i) => ((1+i)(1+i))/((1-i)(1+i))}}
S3. [symbolic] Expand numerator and denominator -> {{((1+i)(1+i))/((1-i)(1+i)) => (1+2i+i^2)/(1-i^2)}}
S4. [symbolic] Use i^2 = -1 in the numerator and denominator -> {{(1+2i+i^2)/(1-i^2) => (2i)/2}}
S5. [symbolic] Reduce the fraction -> {{(2i)/2 => i}}
S6. [symbolic] Substitute z = i into the power -> {{((1+i)/(1-i))^1000 => i^1000}}
S7. [theorem] The powers of i repeat with period 4, and i^(4k) = 1 for every integer k.
S8. [calc] Reduce the exponent modulo 4 -> <<1000 mod 4 = 0>>
S9. [symbolic] Apply the periodicity since the remainder is 0 -> {{i^1000 => 1}}

[Check]
V1. Reverse the simplification: i^1000 = (i^4)^250 = 1^250 = 1, which agrees with the result.
V2. Since 1000/4 = 250 exactly, the exponent is a multiple of 4, so i^1000 = 1.

[Answer]
\boxed{1}

