solution:
[Overview]
This is a rational equation problem. The goal is to find the smallest real solution. The key idea is to note the denominator restrictions, combine fractions, clear denominators, solve the resulting quadratic, and check the valid roots.

[Plan]
P1. State the values excluded by the denominators.
P2. Combine the two fractions on the left side.
P3. Clear denominators and simplify to a quadratic equation.
P4. Solve the quadratic equation.
P5. Compare the roots and choose the smallest allowed root.
P6. Verify the chosen root in the original equation.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] The denominators require x != 2, x != 3, and x != 4.
S2. [symbolic] Combine the left side over a common denominator -> {{1/(x-2)+1/(x-4) => (2x-6)/((x-2)(x-4))}}
S3. [symbolic] Factor the numerator on the left -> {{(2x-6)/((x-2)(x-4)) => 2(x-3)/((x-2)(x-4))}}
S4. [symbolic] Since x != 3, cross-multiply the equation -> {{2(x-3)/((x-2)(x-4)) = 3/(x-3) => 2(x-3)^2 = 3(x-2)(x-4)}}
S5. [symbolic] Expand and collect terms -> {{2(x-3)^2 = 3(x-2)(x-4) => x^2 - 6x + 6 = 0}}
S6. [symbolic] Apply the quadratic formula -> {{x^2 - 6x + 6 = 0 => x = (6 +/- sqrt(12))/2 = 3 +/- sqrt(3)}}
S7. [symbolic] Compare the two roots -> {{3 - sqrt(3) < 3 + sqrt(3) => smallest allowed solution is 3 - sqrt(3)}}

[Check]
V1. The root 3 - sqrt(3) is about 1.268, so it is not equal to 2, 3, or 4.
V2. Let s = sqrt(3) and x = 3 - s. Then 1/(x-2)+1/(x-4)=1/(1-s)-1/(1+s)=-s and 3/(x-3)=-3/s=-s, so the original equation holds.

[Answer]
\boxed{3-\sqrt{3}}

