solution:
[Overview]
This is an algebraic simplification problem. The goal is to simplify the given expression and write it in the form ap-b. The key idea is to simplify each parenthesized factor, expand, and combine like terms.

[Plan]
P1. Simplify the expression inside the first set of parentheses.
P2. Multiply the simplified first part by 3.
P3. Simplify the expression inside the second set of parentheses.
P4. Expand the product in the second part.
P5. Add the two simplified parts and combine like terms.
P6. Verify the result with a sample substitution.

[Solution]
S1. [symbolic] Simplify the first parentheses -> {{(5p+1)-2p*4 => -3p+1}}
S2. [symbolic] Multiply the first simplified factor by 3 -> {{(-3p+1)*3 => -9p+3}}
S3. [symbolic] Simplify the second parentheses -> {{4-1/3 => 11/3}}
S4. [symbolic] Expand the second product -> {{(11/3)*(6p-9) => 22p-33}}
S5. [symbolic] Add the two parts and combine like terms -> {{(-9p+3)+(22p-33) => 13p-30}}

[Check]
V1. Substitute p=1 into the original expression -> <<((5*1+1)-2*1*4)*3+(4-1/3)*(6*1-9)=-17>>
V2. Substitute p=1 into the simplified expression -> <<13*1-30=-17>>

[Answer]
\boxed{13p-30}

